    {
      "id": "18",
      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 18,
        "body_ja": null,
        "body": "<p>In Japanese, <span data-gp-id='21'>を</span> is a particle that marks the object of an action. This means that the <span data-gp-id='10'>subject</span> (the person or thing performing an action) is performing that action with the <span data-gp-id='21'>object</span> being the goal/target of the action. In most cases, whatever <span data-gp-id='21'>を</span> is attached to will not actually be 'doing' anything, but will instead have something being done to it.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154747'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154748'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>In these sentences, <span data-gp-id='21'>を</span> is marking the thing that is having an action performed 'on' it. This is how the Japanese language views the '<span data-gp-id='21'>object</span>'. However, unlike English, an <span data-gp-id='21'>object</span> can also be a place in which an action is performed 'through', if the goal of that action is based in that location itself.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154749'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154750'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>In these sentences, <span data-gp-id='21'>を</span> is marking the place 'through' which the action is taking place. This use of <span data-gp-id='21'>を</span> highlights that the place itself is what is being interacted with (running/walking requires interaction with the ground, so the ground/place is the object).</p>"
      }
    },
