    {
      "id": "1199",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1199,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "が and を are basic Japanese particles. が marks the subject—the one doing the action, while を marks the direct object, the thing being acted upon. For example, ジョンがリンゴを食べた means 'John ate an apple.' が is also used in expressions of preference or ability, like コーヒーが好きです ('I like coffee') or 英語がわかります ('I understand English').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 21,
          "slug": "を",
